": "pyyaml", "version": "6.0.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "pyyaml-env-tag", "version": "1.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "pyzmq", "version": "27.2.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "referencing", "version": "0.37.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "regex", "version": "2026.9.29", "vulns": []}, {"name": "requests", "version": "2.34.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "rfc3339-validator", "version": "0.1.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "rfc3986-validator", "version": "0.1.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "rfc3987-syntax", "version": "1.1.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "rich", "version": "15.0.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "rich-click", "version": "1.9.9", "vulns": []}, {"name": "rpds-py", "version": "2026.9.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "ruff", "version": "0.17.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "scikit-learn", "version": "1.5.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "scipy", "version": "1.17.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "seaborn", "version": "0.13.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "send2trash", "version": "2.1.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "setuptools", "version": "70.3.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "shellingham", "version": "1.5.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "six", "version": "1.17.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "sklearn-compat", "version": "0.1.6", "vulns": []}, {"name": "skops", "version": "0.16.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "smmap", "version": "5.0.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "sniffio", "version": "1.3.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "sortedcontainers", "version": "2.4.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "soupsieve", "version": "3.0.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "sqlalchemy", "version": "2.1.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "sqlparse", "version": "0.6.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "stack-data", "version": "0.6.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "starlette", "version": "1.7.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "statsmodels", "version": "0.15.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "stevedore", "version": "5.9.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "tenacity", "version": "9.2.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "terminado", "version": "0.18.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "threadpoolctl", "version": "3.7.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "tinycss2", "version": "1.5.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "tomli", "version": "2.5.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "tomli-w", "version": "1.2.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "tornado", "version": "6.5.10", "vulns": [{"id": "CVE-2026-116577", "fix_versions": ["6.6"], "aliases": ["GHSA-pcg2-g6cj-q92c"], "description": "### Summary  Tornado's HTTP header parser takes quadratic time (O(n\u00b2)) when a header value is continued across many \"obsolete line folding\" (obs-fold) lines. An unauthenticated attacker can send a request whose header block is mostly short continuation lines. Parsing it uses a large amount of CPU on the IOLoop thread and blocks every other connection that process is serving.  This issue is closely related to CVE-2025-67725 (GHSA-c98p-7wgm-6p64). The fix for that advisory covered repeated headers but not line-folded continuations.  ### Impact  All Tornado HTTP servers are affected. Header parsing runs before any application code, so no authentication or particular handler is required.  How bad it gets depends on `max_header_size`:  - **Default (64 KB):** each malicious request blocks the event loop for tens of milliseconds. Repeated requests can degrade service. - **Raised `max_header_size`:** the cost grows with the square of the header size. A single 1 MB request can block the process for several seconds.  Only availability is affected. Confidentiality and integrity are not.  ### Patches  Fixed in Tornado 6.6 by removing support for obsolete line folding completely; header lines that begin with whitespace are now rejected.  ### Workarounds  - Keep `max_header_size` at its default value or lower it. This limits the impact but doesn't remove it. - Run Tornado behind a reverse proxy that rejects or normalizes obsolete line folding before forwarding requests.  ### Credit  Reported by @manus-pi."}, {"id": "CVE-2026-115927", "fix_versions": ["6.6"], "aliases": ["GHSA-r4j5-9xpp-3985"], "description": "### Summary  When Tornado reads an HTTP/1.1 body that uses chunked transfer encoding, it processes all chunks that have already arrived without yielding to the event loop. A client can send a body made of millions of 1-byte chunks, which blocks the event loop (and therefore every other connection served by that process) for the entire time it takes to parse the body. A body near the default `max_body_size` of 100 MB can block the process for close to a minute, and the attacker can repeat or parallelize these requests.  Bodies made of tiny chunks also consumed far more memory than their size: a handler that does not use `@stream_request_body` stored each chunk as a separate object, using roughly 20 times as much memory as the bytes received.  ### Impact  All Tornado HTTP servers are affected when they receive a request body for a handler that reads it, which includes any handler that does not reject the request before the body is read. No authentication is required. Lowering `max_body_size` reduces the maximum blocking time and memory use for each request but does not remove the issue.  `SimpleAsyncHTTPClient` is affected in the same way when it reads a response from a malicious server.  ### Patches  Fixed in Tornado 6.6. Tornado now yields to the event loop periodically while reading any body, combines small chunks before passing them to `data_received`, and stores non-streaming bodies more compactly.  Applications using `@stream_request_body` should note that the pieces passed to `data_received` no longer correspond to HTTP chunk boundaries.  ### Workarounds  - Run Tornado behind a reverse proxy that buffers request bodies and forwards them with a `Content-Length` header instead of chunked encoding. - Lower `max_body_size` (for the whole server or for individual handlers) to limit the impact of each request.  ### Credit  Reported by [uttej-badwane](https://github.com/uttej-badwane)."}, {"id": "CVE-2026-116578", "fix_versions": ["6.6"], "aliases": ["GHSA-pw9p-v7r6-gx4p"], "description": "### Summary  `tornado.web.RedirectHandler` builds its target URL by substituting parts of the request path into a template. Depending on where those substitutions appear in the template, an attacker can craft a request that redirects to a host of their choosing. This creates an open redirect on the application's domain, which can be used in phishing.  ### Impact  Applications are affected if they use `RedirectHandler` with a URL template where a regex capture group can influence the scheme or host of the redirect. This includes:  - **A substitution at the start of the path**, for example `(r\"/(.*)/target\", RedirectHandler, {\"url\": \"/{0}/destination\"})`. A request to `https://example.com//evil.com/target` redirects to `//evil.com/destination`, a protocol-relative URL pointing to evil.com. Variants using backslashes or encoded characters have the same effect, because browsers treat them as equivalent to `//`. - **A substitution directly after the host**, for example `(r\"/old(.*)\", RedirectHandler, {\"url\": \"https://new.example.com{0}\"})`. A request to `/old.evil.com/` or `/old@evil.com/` redirects to evil.com. - **A template that begins with a substitution** (such as `\"{0}\"`), which can be given an absolute URL.  Applications are not affected if their `RedirectHandler` templates use only static URLs, or if every substitution comes after a fixed host and a `/` (for example `\"/static/{0}\"` or `\"https://example.com/{0}\"`). Other uses of `RequestHandler.redirect()` are not affected by this advisory, although applications that pass user-controlled input to `redirect()` should validate it themselves.  ### Patches  Fixed in Tornado 6.6. `RedirectHandler` now returns a 400 error if the substituted values would change the scheme or host of the redirect from what the template specifies. As a result, substitutions can no longer be used in the scheme or host part of a template. The similar check in `StaticFileHandler` has also been hardened against backslash and whitespace variants.  ### Workarounds  - Change `RedirectHandler` templates so every substitution comes after a fixed prefix ending in `/`, or write capture-group regexes that cannot match characters such as `/`, `\\`, `@`, `.` or `:` where they could affect the host. - Alternatively, replace `RedirectHandler` with a custom handler that validates the target before calling `self.redirect()`.  ### Credit  Reported by Longwei Wang (@gn00295120)."}]}, {"name": "tqdm", "version": "4.70.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "traitlets", "version": "5.16.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "typer", "version": "0.27.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "typing-extensions", "version": "4.16.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "typing-inspect", "version": "0.9.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "typing-inspection", "version": "0.4.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "tzdata", "version": "2026.5", "vulns": []}, {"name": "ujson", "version": "6.0.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "uri-template", "version": "1.3.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "urllib3", "version": "2.8.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "uuid6", "version": "2025.0.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "uvicorn", "version": "0.54.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "uvloop", "version": "0.23.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "watchdog", "version": "6.0.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "watchfiles", "version": "1.3.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "wcwidth", "version": "0.9.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "webcolors", "version": "25.10.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "webencodings", "version": "0.6.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "websocket-client", "version": "1.9.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "websockets", "version": "17.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "werkzeug", "version": "3.1.9", "vulns": []}, {"name": "widgetsnbextension", "version": "4.0.16", "vulns": []}, {"name": "wrapt", "version": "2.5.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "xgboost", "version": "3.2.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "yarl", "version": "1.25.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "zipp", "version": "4.1.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "-e -", "skip_reason": "could not deduce package version from URL requirement"}], "fixes": []}
